# pi-crust environment. Copy to .env at the target repo root and fill in.
#
# WHICH KEYS YOU NEED DEPENDS ON YOUR ROSTER.
# Every `model:` in .pi-crust/config/crust.config.yaml is written as provider/model-id.
# The provider half decides which key must be set, and which key pi reads for
# that provider comes from ~/.pi/agent/models.json.
#
# The starter roster names three providers, so it needs three keys:
#   google/gemini-3.6-flash                      served via openrouter -> OPENROUTER_API_KEY
#   fireworks/accounts/fireworks/models/kimi-k3  fireworks             -> FIREWORKS_API_KEY
#   openai/gpt-5.6-terra, openai/gpt-5.6-luna    openai                -> OPENAI_API_KEY
#
# Point every agent at one provider and you only need that provider's key.
# Setting `defaults.model` and deleting the per-agent `model:` overrides is the
# fastest way to run the whole roster on a single key.
#
# Nothing validates this for you. `validateAgents()` checks that a model is
# written as provider/id, not that the provider is reachable or that its key is
# set, so a missing key shows up when that agent runs, not at startup.

OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
FIREWORKS_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Everything below is optional. Each has a working default, and every one of
# them is also a flag on the command that reads it — a flag wins over the
# variable, because a flag is typed for one run and an exported variable
# outlives the terminal it was set in.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# WHERE THINGS ARE

# CRUST_CONFIG=                    # roster to run, default .pi-crust/config/crust.config.yaml (--config wins)
# CRUST_DB=                        # trace to read or write, default .pi-crust/data/crust.db (--db wins)
#                                  # this is how the reporter and the observer are pointed at another repo
# CRUST_HOME=                      # where pi-crust itself is installed. The installer records this in
#                                  # .pi-crust/data/pi-crust-home; set it when the package has moved
# CRUST_REGISTRY=                  # the machine's list of trees that have run, default ~/.pi-crust/workspaces.jsonl
# PI_PATH=pi                       # pi binary, if not on PATH
# PI_MODELS_PATH=                  # default ~/.pi/agent/models.json

# HOW A RUN BEHAVES

# CRUST_TRUST_LEVEL=               # none | low | medium | high, default medium (--trust-level wins)
#                                  # medium answers a reversible question it can defend, says so, and lists
#                                  # every such answer at the end. It still stops for a question it cannot
#                                  # answer, and for anything reaching beyond what discarding the run undoes.
#                                  # none leaves nothing to judgement; high lets no question stop the run.
#                                  # Numbers 0-100 still parse — they are the storage format — but the dial
#                                  # has four behaviours, so 80 and 60 are the same run as 50.
# ENGINEER_NAME=                   # the engineer lane's label, defaults to git user.name

# THE OBSERVER

# CRUST_BE_PORT=4600               # the API behind the UI
# CRUST_FE_PORT=4601               # the web app itself — this is the one you open
# CRUST_TOKEN=                     # REQUIRED BEFORE THE UI CAN WRITE ANYTHING. Unset, every write endpoint
#                                  # answers 503 and says so: approving a gate, steering, asking a step a
#                                  # question and aborting a run are all refused. Reading never needs it.
#                                  # Set the same value here and in the browser. It fails closed by design.
# CRUST_WORKSPACES=                # watch named trees instead of the registered ones:
#                                  # "label=/path/to/tree,other=/path/to/other". A path alone is labelled
#                                  # after its directory. Set, this REPLACES discovery rather than adding to it.
# CRUST_ALL_WORKTREES=             # any non-empty value sweeps every git worktree for a trace. A repair
#                                  # tool, not the normal path — a tree registers itself when it first runs.
